To disable copy elision in C++, you must pass specific compiler flags during compilation. However, your ability to completely turn it off depends heavily on the C++ standard version you are targeting.

1. Compiler Flags

• GCC / Clang: Use the flag -fno-elide-constructors.
• MSVC (Visual Studio 2022 v17.4+): Use the flag /Zc:nrvo-. Note that this explicitly disables optional Named Return Value Optimization (NRVO).


2. The Catch: Mandatory vs. Optional Elision

How these flags behave changes drastically based on whether you are compiling with C++14 or older versus C++17 or newer.

NOTE:   If running using code runner extension like play button in Visual Studio Code
        the changes has to be made in 

        ctl+Shift+P 
        -> 
        Preferences: Open User Settings(JSON) 
        -> 
        "code-runner.executorMap": {"cpp": "cd $dir && g++ -std=c++20 -fno-elide-constructors -Wall $fileName -o $fileNameWithoutExt && $dir$fileNameWithoutExt"},


Adding -fno-elide-constructors as a compilation argument disables Copy elision